{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791695991,"nonce":"tYViK/WOsEbzk1jJPmWRa/X0ptk=","title":"A window drew a frame before laying out what an event had just changed","message":"Clicking the wiki's Count button crashed its window with \"Compute layout before painting or hit testing\".\n\nA window runs a frame in the same turn as the input events it handles. What an event changes is queued until the layout flushes, and only a flush reports that layout changed, so the painter, which lays out only when it has been told, skipped layout and drew; drawing flushed the queue, and layout was stale.\n\nReproduced offscreen by running the painter's steps with an event handler writing text and the next frame following at once, and with the wiki's reactive page by pressing Count.\n\nFix: the painter's layout steps are FrameLayout, which flushes first so the queue's changes are reported before it decides. window.ts uses it. tests/native-frame.mjs clicks in code and draws at once, and adapters/test/wiki.mjs replays a session on the reactive page frame by frame; each fails without the flush.","files":null}]}